      "writtenOn": "2023-02-22T22:29:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "i see we have done this in similar drivers.\nthere is no evidence in the datasheets that this a valid\nsequence to wait for the chip to come out of reset.\n\nthe datasheet just says the chip takes 30ms to do\npower-on-reset and the timing diagram labels I2C\nduring this period as \"invalid\".\nit would be safer to delay 30ms before attempting to\naccess the chip.\n\non the other hand we probably don\u0027t get here until after\nboard_reset_pd_mcu() where we implement the post-reset\ndelay(s)... albeit specific to the ps8815.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 91,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 91,
        "endChar": 65
      },
      "revId": "c7e924f86708960cb2a245ef03e8846211985b01",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fd1f1190_6d1e964f",
        "filename": "driver/retimer/anx7452.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 91,
      "author": {
        "id": 1541242
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-02-23T08:28:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ok, in the programming guide of Analogix, during register initialization it says wait for 30 to 50 ms. If I move the wait to before chipset state check, will it impact the boot time in any way. As it mentioned 30 to 50 I kept 50, do you think 30 ms is fine. For the other Analogix retimer(ANX7483), it\u0027s 30 ms in guide and in code.",
      "parentUuid": "3118c4b9_09c15da4",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 91,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 91,
        "endChar": 65
      },
      "revId": "c7e924f86708960cb2a245ef03e8846211985b01",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1906aad4_c553d3ac",
        "filename": "driver/retimer/anx7452.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 91,
      "author": {
        "id": 1190764
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-02-24T02:35:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "what is the significance of CHIPSET_STATE_HARD_OFF anyway?\nare we assuming the anx7452 is on the same power rail as the AP?\n\nit kind of doesn\u0027t matter because the timing diagram is relative\nto USB_EN. if i\u0027m reading the schematics correctly, that\u0027s\ncontrolled by the EC signal gpio_usb_c1_rt_rst_r_odl.\n\nwe need to update board_reset_pd_mcu() to do the right thing\ndepending on which daughterboard is present and add the\n30 ms delay there if we have a daughterboard with the 7452.\n\ngiven that, i don\u0027t think we need a delay here at all.\nif this driver were responsible for power sequencing or\nresetting the 7452, it would make sense here. as it stands,\nboard_reset_pd_mcu() is where that magic happens.\n\nas for boot time, we have to be a bit careful about which boot\ntime we mean. the EC is still running in RW when the AP is off\nso these delays are not applied when we typically boot the AP\nwhich is where we are expected to meet the 1 second hard limit.",
      "parentUuid": "fd1f1190_6d1e964f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 91,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 91,
        "endChar": 65
      },
      "revId": "c7e924f86708960cb2a245ef03e8846211985b01",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
